      "generated_answer": "In the scenario where a player attempts to perform a Lateral Group Transfer, the rule specifies that such a transfer must be directly opposite an opposing group. The rule does not explicitly mention that a player must announce the intention to perform a Lateral Group Transfer when playing a sideways movement card. However, it is implied that the announcement of the intention to perform such a transfer should be clear to avoid any misunderstandings during gameplay.\n\nFrom the provided rules, Rule 17.7 states: \"A group can attempt a Lateral Group Transfer to a position directly opposite an opposing group.\"\n\nGiven that the German player only has groups at identity chit positions A and B, if the American player's group C attempts a Lateral Group Transfer, it would be illegal to transfer to a position opposite a non-existent German group at D (since the German player's group D does not exist).\n\nAdditionally, Rule 17.1 clarifies: \"Flank status is declared at the time the movement card is played, and is conditional based on the position of the groups involved at the time the card is played.\"\n\nFrom this, it is reasonable to deduce that the player must declare their intention to perform a Lateral Group Transfer when playing the sideways movement card. This declaration would involve placing the new group identity chit in the adjacent column and taking it away if the transfer fails due to the absence of an opposing group.\n\nThere is no rule explicitly stating that a player can change their mind after declaring the intention to perform a Lateral Group Transfer, so we can assume that once declared, the player is committed to the move unless the transfer fails due to the conditions not being met.\n\nTherefore, the player cannot perform a Lateral Group Transfer when there is no opposing group in the direct opposite position, and once the intention is declared, the player must follow through unless the conditions are not met, in which case the transfer fails and the player's group remains in its original position.\n\n[Source: UF RuleBook updated.pdf | Priority: P1 | Rule: 17.7, 17.1]",
      "ground_truth_answer": "In the General Vol 21 Number 1 scenario A series replay, on turn 5 the American plays a sideways movement card on his group C \"as a Lateral Group Transfer\". But given the German player only has groups at identity chit A and B isn't this illegal? Rule 17.7 first sentence says \"... directly opposite an opposing group ...\"\n\nNo, it says \"A group capable of movement can change its group identity and thereby its position directly opposing an enemy group....\"  All it is saying if you move group from B to C, the directly opposing group would now be C--if it exists.  If the opponent  does not have a Group C, then there would be no directly opposing group to your Group C.  (Just like there would be if each side set up with a different number of groups at start.)\n\nIt is not stating a requirement, but a change of status of \"directly opposing\" groups.\n\nThe rule states the player announces the intention to perform a lateral group transfer so presumably if the player doesn't make the announcement at the time of playing the movement card sideways he can't subsequently do so on a future turn (using that same played movement card) even if the necessary conditions still exist to make such a move? \n\nCorrect, if the intention to change a group's ID is not made when playing the movement cards, when group enters terrain, the same Group ID remains.  Very common if you want to get into better terrain.\n\nWhere a game is played without aid of voice or text chat I guess you might agree that a lateral group transfer announcement was accompanied by putting down the new group identity chit in the adjacent column and taken away if the attempt failed.\n\nA common technique in ftf play is to place the new ID on the Movement card until new terrain is accepted or rejected (which would cancel the transfer).\n\nI wonder whether having declared the intention to lateral group transfer a player can change their mind? \n\nI would say no per errata on Rule 5:  Movement cards not played directly to the discard pile cannot voluntarily be cancelled.  (Just like you can't rotate a Movement card from advancing to sideways because you change your mind about advancing.)\n\nEdit: Looking through past posts I see I have broached this issue before! See https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/1342845/article/35597336#35597336 where no conclusion was reached :)\n\nNo big deal, that thread started 11 years ago and you added your question 5 years after that.",
