      "generated_answer": "Regarding your first question about whether a stunned, non-OT AFV must button up:\n\n- The relevant rule is 28.45 [Source: UF RuleBook updated.pdf], which states: \"A Stunned AFV may not move nor fire while under a Stunned status. While stunned, an AFV uses the Button Up rectangle printed on its card, and removes any accumulated Rally credits an already pinned/stunned AFV may have in play.\" The rule explicitly mentions that a stunned AFV uses the Button Up rectangle on its card, indicating that a stunned non-OT AFV is indeed forced to button up.\n\nTherefore, a stunned non-OT AFV must button up as per the rule stated.\n\nRegarding your second question about boxed attacks and their known results:\n\n- The rule for boxed attacks is covered in 28.7 [Source: UF RuleBook updated.pdf]: \"All Personality cards have an inherent boxed firepower of 1 at Relative Range 5 which can be used against an Open Topped AFV's boxed defence value in addition to using their normal firepower at that range against the AFV's unboxed defence value using a second RNC for the combat resolution.\"\n\nWhen you use a boxed attack number against an AFV, you indeed know the exact result if you hit, based on the comparison of your boxed firepower to the AFV's boxed defence value. This contrasts with the usual uncertainty of outcomes in Up Front, where the result of a hit is often determined by drawing a Random Number Card (RNC).\n\nYour understanding of boxed attacks is correct: the result is known upon hitting based on the relative boxed values, which does feel unusual compared to the generally random nature of combat outcomes in Up Front.",
      "ground_truth_answer": "Couple of things - about getting AFVs to button up, one way for infantry to reduce the threat of an AFV is to fire at it with small arms, hoping to get a hit equal to or higher than its Morale value, which forces it to button up.  This is covered by 28.3:\n\nAn AFV card has two Armor defense values printed on each side of the card plus a Morale and CE Defense value on the front of the card. The Armor and Flank defense values represent the armored defense strength and is listed on both sides of the card; only attacks using solely boxed firepower or boxed \"Effect Numbers\" apply to this value. The MORALE and CE values are affected only by unboxed attack values.\n\nThis is how mortars can attack AFVs (and they have a small chance of damaging the vehicle as well [26.5]).  AT guns have the choice of firing AP - the process the OP referred to in his second question, which attacks the Armor/Flank values - or HE, which attacks the Morale value as above.  The HE attack is only possible, of course, when the vehicle is CE, or in the case of an OT vehicle if it is not already Pinned (except for one or two really weedy AFVs like the British Bren Carrier, which has a Panic value and can thus be attacked when Pinned).\n\nAnd moving on to the OPs second question, you were right to feel that there should be a random element is resolving an AP hit - and there is!  25.4 says:\n\nOnce a hit has been obtained, the strength of that hit is determined by adding the To Hit Random Number on the RNC just drawn to the Effect Number listed on the ordnance card. (EXC: IG/AFV ordnance firing at an AFV target does not add the To Hit Random Number just drawn; instead it adds a number equal to the Relative Range between the firer and the target.)  The color of the RNC has no effect; all numbers are added. Having now determined the total strength of the attack (after modification for terrain and/or movement), it is resolved separately for each target in the defending group by drawing a new RNC and adding any black number or subtracting any red number drawn as per a normal fire attack [6.5]. \n\nSo the Hit process establishes first whether a hit has been obtained, and secondly how damaging it is: for HE, the RNC on the To Hit card is used (provided a 0 would have hit, reflecting how close the HE round actually landed), and for AP a hit is a hit, but the RR is added to reflect increased penetration at closer ranges.  This gives you the base hit strength; then, in both cases, you draw an RNC for each target.  Firing at an AFV, of course, there is only one target, but you still draw an RNC.\n\nSo, a Panther (s7,i8,k9) rumbles up to RR4 to a plucky British 6-pdr (FE 3).  If the AT gun gets a hit, its base Hit Strength will be 3+4=7, and then it hopes the RNC will be at least 0, and preferably a black 2 or better to get a kill.\n\nDon't forget, also, that unlike small arms and HE fire against an AFV, terrain does affect the resolution.  So if the AFV is in Brush, the Hit Strength is reduced by 1.  I suppose this reflects the possibility of the round being deflected or slowed by intervening obstacles.\n\nHope all that helps!  Oh, and Snipers are good, too.",
