      "generated_answer": "Based on the rules provided, Natural Flanking Fire (NFF) is considered a type of Flanking Fire and therefore is subject to the same conditions under which Flanking Fire is lost.\n\nAccording to the rule [17.4], Flanking Fire is lost if the flanking group plays a Movement card, which implies that NFF would also be lost under similar conditions. However, NFF specifically requires a group to be moving forward from Relative Range 5 (RR5) to RR4 (RR chit 6). \n\nIf a group at NFF plays a Movement card and does not move forward, it would lose NFF as per the conditions in [17.4]. The exact wording in [17.4] states that Flanking Fire is lost when the flanking group plays a Movement card, which applies to NFF as well since NFF is a form of Flanking Fire as mentioned in [17.3].\n\nAs for regaining NFF, the conditions would need to be met again, which means the group would need to be moving forward from RR5 to RR4. Therefore, if a group was previously at RR4 (RR chit 6) but lost NFF by playing a Movement card, they would regain NFF only if they move back to RR5 and then move forward to RR4 again.\n\nTo summarize:\n- NFF is lost if a group at NFF plays a Movement card and does not move forward.\n- NFF is regained only when the group moves forward from RR5 to RR4.\n\nIf you have any more specific questions or need further clarification, please let me know. This text has changed across versions. Would you like to know how it worked in a specific version, or how it has evolved over time?",
